Start only the mat-vec threads that have a block to read.

The k-quant integer mat-vec shader runs in workgroups of 256 threads. A
thread reads 16 values of the row, so a row of K values has work for
K / 16 threads. The matrices of PLaMo 3 Translate 0.8B are 1536, 2048 or
6144 values wide, so for most of them only 96 or 128 of the 256 threads
compute anything. The rest still take part in the reduction at the end.

The workgroup is now sized to the row, rounded up to whole subgroups and
never above 256: 96 threads for K = 1536, 128 for K = 2048 and 256 as
before for K = 6144. The 2B model gets 160 and 192. A thread reads the
same block as before, and whole subgroups are kept so that the sums are
still added in the same order. The shader source is the same and only
its WG_SIZE define changes, so the pipeline key now carries the size.

This applies only with the subgroup reduction. The workgroup reduction
folds the workgroup in halves and drops part of the sum when the size is
not a power of two. A build without this condition, run with the
subgroups feature hidden, used 96 threads there and every request failed
with invalid UTF-8 in the output. That path keeps 256 threads.

Only NVIDIA with Q4_K and Q6_K rows gets the smaller workgroup. That is
what was measured, on an RTX 4090 with a subgroup size of 32. Both
models translated to identical text, and with the 0.8B model a long
article took about 12% less time in the extension.

Other vendors and other quant types keep 256 threads. This is not a
claim about them.

